# Development — Readiness Check

The last gate before code. It confirms the whole spec chain hangs together —
requirements → design → tasks — and is approved, so implementation does not stall
on a gap discovered mid-story.

## Goal

Produce a readiness verdict (PASS / CONCERNS / FAIL) with evidence, so the
development lead either starts `tdd-cycle` with confidence or fixes a specific,
named gap first.

## Default Operation Mode

Direct assessment, read-only. You evaluate and report; you do not fix what you
find — you hand specific gaps back to the phase that owns them.

## Collect Inputs First

- Which feature? Confirm `specs/<feature>/` has requirements, design, and tasks.
- Run `npx -y @protonspy/csdd spec status <feature>` and `npx -y @protonspy/csdd spec validate <feature>` to get the
  mechanical state before adding judgment.

## Execution Workflow

### 1) Mechanical gate
Run `npx -y @protonspy/csdd spec validate <feature>`. Any non-zero exit is an automatic FAIL until
fixed — record the exact issues.

### 2) Coherence checks
Walk `assets/readiness-report-template.md`:
- Every requirement ID appears in the design traceability table.
- Every design boundary has at least one task; every task cites requirements.
- No task depends on a boundary that does not exist.
- Acceptance criteria are testable (a `tdd-cycle` RED test could be written).

### 3) Context checks
- Steering (`tech.md`, `structure.md`) reflects the chosen architecture.
- External dependencies / MCP servers the tasks assume are actually configured.
- The test command and verification path are known (see `verify-change`).

### 4) Verdict
Grade each section and produce an overall verdict with the blocking items listed
first. Write `specs/<feature>/readiness.md`.

## Gotchas

- A green `npx -y @protonspy/csdd spec validate` is necessary but not sufficient — it checks
  structure, not whether the plan is actually buildable. Do the judgment checks.
- CONCERNS is not PASS. If you would not want to be paged for it later, it is at
  least a CONCERN.
- Name the owning phase for every gap (discovery / architecture / stories) so the
  fix is unambiguous.
- Read-only: do not edit specs to make them pass. Report and route.

## Verification Before Reporting

- Run: `npx -y @protonspy/csdd spec validate <feature>` and `npx -y @protonspy/csdd spec status <feature>`.
- Check: every checklist item is graded with evidence; the overall verdict
  matches the worst blocking item.
- If blocked: if you cannot determine readiness (missing test command, unknown
  dependency), that uncertainty is itself a CONCERN — state it.

## Completion Criteria
- [ ] `specs/<feature>/readiness.md` exists with a graded checklist.
- [ ] Mechanical validation result is recorded (and FAIL if non-zero).
- [ ] Each gap names the phase that must fix it.
- [ ] An overall PASS / CONCERNS / FAIL verdict is stated; PASS is required to
      start `tdd-cycle`.

## References

- Use `assets/readiness-report-template.md` for the checklist and verdict.
